LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — Every exit computes once", "12", "<p>The track's machinery has computed the same population month after month; this module governs the computation that runs ONCE per person, ever: the exit — the full and final settlement — and its thesis carries the module's whole weight: <b>every exit computes once — from documents, on a deadline, with dignity.</b></p><p><b>Why exits are the hardest computation.</b> The F&amp;F is one person's ENTIRE account settled in a single pass: the last salary prorated to a day, the leave ledger closed to a naira, the notice terms resolved, every open instrument settled, the statutory layer applied one final time — with no next month to correct into (module 5's forward-arrears channel closes when the person walks out: the exit computation is the one this track cannot repair later, which is why it borrows every discipline the track built and applies them simultaneously, under a deadline, to a person who is leaving). And the stakes exceed the arithmetic: the leaver's settlement is the firm's LAST word to them — the exit handled cleanly is the story they tell at the next employer, the trade association, and the reference call (HR 7's reference commons, fed from this desk); the exit fumbled is the grievance that outlives the employment, sometimes into a tribunal, always into the trade's memory. The payroll function's reputation is made at two moments — payday and exit day — and this module is the second one.</p><p><b>The inputs: HR 7's package, consumed.</b> The boundary's last crossing, received: the HR track's separation checklist hands this module its clean inputs — the LAST WORKING DAY confirmed (the date everything prorates from), the LEAVE BALANCE from the ledger (the days this module will value), the NOTICE status (served, paid in lieu, or shortfall — per the contract's terms), the DOCUMENTED deductions (the loan balances with their separation clauses — module 4's instruments cashing their hardest clause), and the settlement's context (the exit's nature — resignation, dismissal, redundancy, retirement, death — because Chapters 5-6 will show the nature changes the components). The module's standing gratitude to the HR track, made explicit for the two-certification consultant: the F&amp;F that is a nightmare in the wild is a nightmare because these inputs arrive as recollections — the whole reason HR 7 built the package is so this module can compute instead of negotiate, and the pairing is the trilogy's tightest join.</p><p><b>The module's map.</b> The settlement's anatomy (Chapter 2) and its worked computation (Chapter 3); the process that runs it once, on a deadline (Chapter 4); the exit natures beyond resignation — redundancy and retirement (Chapter 5) and the deceased-employee case (Chapter 6 — the module's hardest chapter, handled with the rigour and humanity it demands); the special-case catalogue that shares this module's edge machinery (Chapter 7 — maternity, unpaid months, suspensions, the mid-month everything); HealthTrade's exits year (Chapter 8); and the seventh law (Chapter 9). Throughout, the adviser boundary at its brightest — because exit law is where employment law concentrates, and the consultant's identity sentence gets its hardest test and holds: the machinery computes what the professionals confirm.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The settlement's anatomy", "13", "<p>The F&amp;F's components, catalogued — each with its source document and its convention. The anatomy chapter.</p><p><b>The final salary.</b> The last month, prorated: the salary computed to the LAST WORKING DAY per the firm's stated day-count convention (module 1's proration policy — chosen once, applied to every edge, and applying here to its most scrutinised case: the leaver counts the days, and the convention argued at exit is the convention that was never written), with the month's ordinary machinery otherwise intact (the statutory computations on the final earnings, the attendance package's last cargo — the exit month is a month, just shorter).</p><p><b>The leave settlement.</b> HR 4's ledger, closed: the accrued untaken balance VALUED and paid (the encashment at exit — the valuation basis stated in policy: the daily rate derived from the stated base, the same formula every time, because the leave day valued generously for one leaver and thinly for another is the evenness breach at the door), or the NEGATIVE balance recovered (the minus-days of HR 4's governed advance — the unpriced loan priced at last, per the policy the person consented to when the advance was granted: the conversation HR 4 warned about, arriving exactly where it said it would), with the ledger's figure final (the balance disputed at exit is the ledger that was never kept — the firm with HR 4's machinery reads a number; the firm without negotiates a memory).</p><p><b>The notice treatment.</b> The contract's terms, resolved three ways: notice SERVED (the standard case — the final salary simply runs to the last day), notice PAID IN LIEU (the firm ending the relationship faster than the notice — the notice period's pay added to the settlement, computed on the stated basis per the contract and the adviser's confirmation), or the notice SHORTFALL (the leaver departing faster than their notice — the contract's remedy, typically the shortfall's value recoverable from the settlement, applied per the terms and with the adviser's shape where disputed), each per the CONTRACT (module 2 of the HR track set these terms at the offer; this desk reads them, never invents them).</p><p><b>The open instruments.</b> Module 4's consent layer, cashing its separation clauses: the LOAN balance settled per its instrument (the clause drafted at lending — full recovery from the settlement, or the negotiated schedule the clause provides: the instrument answers, which is why Chapter 7 of module 4 insisted on the clause), the salary advance recovered, the asset-related recoveries where documented (the unreturned item's documented value per the acknowledgment — never an improvised deduction, even now: the consent law does not expire at the exit), and the recovery CAP's exit nuance handled per the adviser (the final settlement is the last slip — the cap's protection and the debts' settlement reconciled per the instruments and the law's bounds, decided at design, not at the door).</p><p><b>The statutory finale.</b> Module 2's layer, one last time: PAYE on the settlement's taxable components (the taxable/non-taxable analysis of the settlement's parts — the terminal payments' tax treatment being precisely the adviser-confirmed configuration, because severance-type payments and certain terminal benefits carry distinct treatment under the reformed law, and the consultant configures the confirmed reading rather than guessing at the desk), the final pension contributions computed and remitted on the ordinary clock (the seven working days apply to the last month exactly as the first — the exit does not pause the fiduciary stream), and the person's statutory registers CLOSED complete (the final entries written, the year-to-date totals final — the annual return will carry this person's partial year, and the register that closed clean files it by export like everyone else's).</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The settlement computed: the walk", "14", "<p>The anatomy, made arithmetic: one worked settlement, every stop narrated — the module's version of module 1's gross-to-net walk, at the exit. The computation chapter.</p><p><b>The case.</b> A grade 2 employee — the ₦250,000-gross structure of module 1's walk — resigns with proper notice, last working day the 18th of a 30-day month (the firm's convention: calendar days). Open items: leave balance +6 days, staff loan balance ₦90,000 (instrument's separation clause: full settlement from final pay, schedule negotiable at the firm's discretion), no notice shortfall (notice served). The walk:</p><p><b>Step 1 — the prorated final salary.</b> Gross for 18 of 30 days: ₦250,000 × 18/30 = <b>₦150,000</b> — components prorating together per the structure (the statutory bases prorating with them, by construction).</p><p><b>Step 2 — the leave settlement.</b> The valuation basis (the policy states: gross-derived daily rate on the annual convention — illustratively ₦250,000 × 12 / 365 ≈ ₦8,219 per day): 6 days × ₦8,219 = <b>≈ ₦49,315</b> — added to the settlement, taxable per the confirmed treatment.</p><p><b>Step 3 — the statutory layer on the final earnings.</b> The pension contribution on the prorated base (the flagged trio prorated: 8% of ₦135,000 = ₦10,800 employee share; the employer's ₦13,500 accrued beside it), PAYE per module 2's annual frame (the year-to-date actuals plus this final month's taxable income — the annualised computation running its last instalment for this employment, the configuration handling the part-year per the confirmed method; illustratively ≈ ₦9,600 on this final month's taxable parts), NHF on the prorated basic where in scope.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the instruments.</b> The loan balance per its clause: ₦90,000 settled from the final pay (the balance confirmed against the three-way reconciliation of module 5 — the slip line, the register, the ledger agreeing before the settlement consumes the number).</p><p><b>Step 5 — the settlement.</b> ₦150,000 + ₦49,315 − ₦10,800 − ₦9,600 − (NHF if in scope) − ₦90,000 = <b>≈ ₦88,900 net settlement</b> — every line derived, every source named.</p><p><b>The statement.</b> The walk's output is a DOCUMENT the leaver reads: the settlement statement — each component on its line with its source (the proration's arithmetic shown, the leave days and rate shown, the loan's closing balance referenced to its instrument, the statutory lines named), module 4's explainability standard applied to the person with the most right to it and the least reason to extend trust — and the statement WALKED with the leaver (the sit-down where the computation is narrated, questions answered by pointing at the desk one last time: the meeting that costs twenty minutes and pre-empts the dispute that costs months), the leaver's acknowledgment invited on the statement itself (Chapter 4's discharge layer beginning here). The chapter's craft note for the consultant: this walk, templated, IS the deliverable — the settlement statement format designed at implementation, tested against a worked case exactly like this one, so that every future exit fills a form instead of inventing a document — the F&amp;F made routine by the same move that made everything else in this academy routine: the format built once, in peacetime.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The process: once, on a deadline, discharged", "13", "<p>The computation's shell: the process that runs the settlement once, on time, with its evidence complete. The process chapter.</p><p><b>The settlement as a document through the machinery.</b> The F&amp;F rides the track's standing rails: COMPUTED from the machinery (the settlement generated from the structures, ledgers, registers, and instruments — never a hand-built spreadsheet, because the hand-built settlement is module 3's hand-edited slip at the moment of maximum consequence), APPROVED by separated hands (the preparer-approver law with the settlement's summary — the components, the sources, the net: the exit settlement is a single payment with unified-hands risk written all over it, which is exactly module 5's off-cycle warning, and the separations do not soften because the person is leaving), PAID through the standing separations (the off-cycle mini-run where the timing demands it — the settlement released like every payment: prepared, verified, released, evidenced), and POSTED complete (the final journal — the settlement's components to their addresses, the loan receivable closing to zero, the statutory accruals joining their custody streams, the person's cost ending on their cost center's P&amp;L on the right date).</p><p><b>The deadline.</b> The timeline as policy: the settlement paid within the STATED days of the exit (the firm's policy number, set with the adviser against statutory and practice expectations — and set HONESTLY: the deadline the firm can actually meet, published, then met every time, because the settlement's timeliness is its second-most-remembered property after its correctness), with the deadline's feasibility ENGINEERED by everything upstream (the settlement that takes months in the wild takes days here because the inputs are ledgers, not investigations — HR 7's package plus this track's registers making the deadline a scheduling fact rather than an aspiration), and the exception governed (the genuinely disputed component — the contested notice shortfall, the integrity case's pending recovery — handled by settling the UNDISPUTED bulk on time while the disputed line resolves through its proper channel: the person's clean money never held hostage to the argued line, per the adviser's shape).</p><p><b>The discharge.</b> The settlement's closing evidence: the DISCHARGE acknowledgment — the leaver's signed confirmation that the settlement statement was received, walked, and (per its terms) accepted in settlement of the entitlements it covers (the instrument's exact scope and wording being the adviser's drafting — the discharge is a legal document and the consultant installs its process, not its prose), filed with the settlement (the exit file complete: the statement, the discharge, the payment evidence, the closed registers — the file that answers the tribunal question three years later in five minutes), and the CERTIFICATE of service issued beside it (the factual document — the dates, the roles — that Nigerian practice expects and the leaver's next chapter needs: issued promptly and factually per HR 7's reference stance, the last of the firm's routine duties done routinely).</p><p><b>The exit file's afterlife.</b> The process's tail, closing the chapter: the person's payroll history retained per the retention horizons (module 4's archive law — the ex-employee's slips and settlement retrievable for the years the law and life require: the pension reconciliation that arrives four years later, the reference check, the leaver's own TCC needs — module 2's registers serving their alumni), the status closed in every register (the statutory registers' final entries flagged terminal — the annual return's partial-year rows carrying their exit dates), and the settlement's one-version law absolute (the settlement computed once, discharged once, never recomputed — the post-exit claim tested against the exit file's documents, which is what the file was built to do). The module's process promise, in one line: the firm that runs this chapter settles every exit once, on its published deadline, with a statement the leaver understood and a file the future can read — and that firm's exits are boring, which at this desk is the highest available compliment.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Redundancy & retirement: the terminal natures", "13", "<p>Two exit natures with their own law and their own duties — walked at consultant altitude with the adviser's ownership explicit throughout. The terminal chapter.</p><p><b>Redundancy.</b> The exit the business decides: the role eliminated (the restructure, the branch closure, the downturn) — governed territory in Nigerian employment practice, and the consultant's map is boundaries-first: the LAW's layer (redundancy carries statutory and contractual expectations — the consultation and notification dimensions where they apply, the negotiated redundancy terms Nigerian practice typically settles per contract, policy, or union agreement, and the severance arithmetic per those instruments: ALL adviser terrain — the consultant never computes a redundancy package from folklore, because this is the exit nature most likely to be tested), the MACHINERY's layer (what the system contributes: the selection documentation where criteria applied — the HR track's evidence disciplines serving the fairness record; the package computed per the confirmed terms through Chapter 3's templated walk with its redundancy components added; the tax treatment of the severance elements per the adviser-confirmed configuration — the terminal-payment treatments of Chapter 2's note at their most live), and the CONDUCT layer (the redundancy communicated with HR 7's document-is-the-fairness discipline — told properly, heard, papered: the redundancy done with dignity is remembered as a hard thing done fairly; done casually it is remembered in filings).</p><p><b>Retirement.</b> The exit the calendar decides: the career concluding at the retirement age the terms define — and the module's duties are the pension system's last mile: the FINAL contributions computed and remitted on the ordinary clock (the last month's shares reaching the RSA like every month's — the fiduciary stream's final delivery, verified deliberately because it is the one the person is about to live on), the RSA HANDOVER oriented (the retiree's access to their savings — the programmed withdrawal and annuity pathways PenCom's framework provides — is the PERSON'S process with their PFA: the firm's duty is the clean contribution record and the supporting documents, the consultant's orientation is the signpost, and the retirement-planning specifics are the PFA's and adviser's terrain: the identity sentence holding at the retiree's most consequential conversation), the GROUP LIFE cover confirmed through the last day (module 2's coupled policy — in force until the employment ends, verified, because the gap month is the uninsured month), and the settlement's ordinary components running per Chapters 2-4 (the leave ledger, the final proration — retirement is an exit with ceremony, and the ceremony rides on the machinery being invisible).</p><p><b>The long-service recognition.</b> The policy layer where the firm runs one: the long-service award or gratuity (the retirement gift, the years-of-service payment — a POLICY per module 5's policy-versus-gift law: stated, budgeted per Accounts 4's provision logic where material, taxed per the confirmed treatment, and administered evenly), and the ceremony itself noted as the culture's cheapest high-yield investment: the retirement marked properly — the years named, the thanks meant, the send-off attended — costs an afternoon and teaches every watching employee what the firm's promises are worth at the far end; the retirement that passes unmarked teaches the same lesson with the opposite sign. The chapter's closing frame for both natures: redundancy and retirement are where the firm's employment promises reach their terminal test — and the machinery's job is to make the arithmetic unarguable so the humanity has room to be the story.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The deceased employee: rigour and humanity", "14", "<p>The module's hardest chapter — the death of an employee in service — handled the only acceptable way: with complete procedural rigour and complete humanity, simultaneously. The chapter every consultant hopes the client never needs and every client eventually does.</p><p><b>The immediate duties.</b> The first days, governed in advance so nobody improvises in grief: the record's status handled with care (the death recorded per HR 7's event discipline — the date, the documentation as it arrives), the payroll state FROZEN correctly (the next run's validation catching the status — the deceased must never appear in an ordinary payment file: the credit to a dead person's account is a legal and practical tangle the process exists to prevent; the final entitlements travel the estate channel below), and the family CONTACT assigned to one senior, named person (the firm speaks with one voice, and that voice is chosen for the task — the practical questions the family will have, met with answers, not referrals).</p><p><b>The group life claim.</b> Module 2's coupled policy, earning its entire purpose: the employer's life insurance obligation under the pension framework exists FOR THIS DAY — and the firm's duty is speed and completeness: the claim initiated within DAYS (the policy's notification clock met early — the claim documents assembled from the record layer: the HR track's file having everything the insurer asks for is this moment's dividend), the beneficiary framework the policy names honoured (the nominated beneficiaries from the record — HR 1's next-of-kin and nominee data, captured at onboarding and verified annually, revealing here why the verification ritual mattered: the stale nomination is a family dispute detonated at the worst moment), and the claim TRACKED to payment (the firm's advocacy with the insurer as part of the duty — the claim that stalls gets the firm's weight behind it, because the family should never have to fight the firm's insurer alone).</p><p><b>The pension death benefits.</b> The RSA's afterlife, oriented: the deceased's Retirement Savings Account passes to their beneficiaries through the PENSION framework's process (the PFA's death-benefit procedure under PenCom's rules — the documentation, the beneficiary verification, the legal-representation requirements where they apply), with the firm's role being SUPPORT (the employment records, the final contribution evidence — remitted complete, including the last month's: the register that reconciles through the final month is the family's clean starting point — and the orientation help the family needs to begin the PFA process), and the boundary explicit: the death-benefit process is the PFA's and the family's legal channel — the firm supports, documents, and expedites; it does not administer.</p><p><b>The estate settlement.</b> The final entitlements — the accrued salary, the leave balance, the terminal components — computed by Chapter 3's machinery exactly as any settlement (the arithmetic owed the same rigour: grief is not a reason for a wrong number in either direction) and paid through the LEGAL channel the adviser confirms (the estate, the letters of administration, the next-of-kin frameworks — the recipient question is a legal question with real consequences, and the firm that pays the wrong hands with the best intentions has bought a dispute among the grieving: the adviser is ESSENTIAL here, engaged immediately, and the settlement waits for the channel to be right rather than fast — with the family told the process and its honest timeline, which is itself a kindness).</p><p><b>The conduct.</b> The chapter's real subject, closing it: the family's experience of the firm in these weeks IS the firm's culture, witnessed at its most tested — the speed on the claims, the dignity in the contact, the completeness of the documents, the attendance at the funeral where the relationship warrants it, the workforce supported too (the colleagues' grief acknowledged; the HR track's welfare instincts at their most needed) — and the consultant's contribution, made in peacetime, is the PROCESS: the deceased-employee procedure written before it is needed (the checklist: statuses, claims, contacts, channels, timelines), because the firm with the procedure grieves and executes, while the firm without does neither well — and the module's hardest chapter closes on the academy's oldest conviction wearing its most serious clothes: the structure built in calm is what carries everyone when calm is gone.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The special-case catalogue", "13", "<p>The edge cases that share this module's machinery — each governed by the same move: the convention stated, the computation derived, the communication honest. The catalogue chapter.</p><p><b>Maternity pay.</b> HR 4's entitlement, priced: the maternity leave's pay treatment per the statutory floor and the firm's policy (the paid weeks at the stated rate — Nigerian practice's statutory maternity provisions being the adviser-confirmed floor, the firm's policy meeting or exceeding it: the configuration carrying the confirmed treatment), computed by the machinery (the maternity months' slips running per the treatment — the statutory computations continuing correctly on the paid amounts, the pension stream's treatment per the confirmed reading), and the communication per module 4 (HR 4's months-ahead planning including the PAY conversation — the person entering leave knowing exactly what each month pays, in writing, before the first affected slip: the never-surprised law at a moment that has enough on it already).</p><p><b>The unpaid-leave month.</b> The near-zero slip, done correctly: the unpaid days prorating the month per the convention (the same day-count as every edge), the statutory layer handling the reduced earnings correctly (the PAYE annual frame absorbing the low month — module 2's projection machinery, not a hand-hack; the pension computed on the actual base), the deductions layer PAUSED deliberately where the net cannot carry them (the loan schedule's pause per its instrument's terms, the resumption dated — module 5's governed handling of the net that cannot serve its recoveries, decided by rule), and the slip issued regardless (the near-zero slip is still the month's account — the person sees the arithmetic, which is precisely when the account matters most).</p><p><b>The suspension month.</b> HR 7's neutral holding, paid correctly: the suspension on pay leaving the slip UNCHANGED (the neutral instrument's whole point — the pay that continues is the presumption of innocence made visible; the payroll seat processes nothing special, which is the design), and the rare unpaid variant travelling only per the procedure and the adviser (the pay stopped is a sanction in fact whatever it is called — the boundary bright, the improvised pay-stop refused by the machinery's rails).</p><p><b>The mid-month everything.</b> The compound month — the joiner who gets an increment, the leaver with an acting allowance ending, the transfer mid-cycle: governed by the ONE CONVENTION applied in sequence (each event effective-dated, the month computed as its dated segments per the standing day-count — the machinery deriving what a hand computation would botch: module 5's per-period arithmetic at its busiest), and the consultant's test for any compound case: the slip's segments each traceable to their event — the explainability standard surviving the month from hell, which is what it was built for.</p><p><b>The multi-month absence.</b> The long case — the extended sick leave, the study leave, the sabbatical where policy grants one: the treatment per the policy's stages (the paid portion, the half-pay stage where policy runs one, the unpaid tail — each stage a dated configuration, the transitions announced ahead per module 4), the statutory continuity managed per the confirmed treatments (the pension and the cover through the stages — the questions the adviser answers at design), and the return's re-entry clean (the resumption dated, the first full month ordinary — the absence that ends cleanly in the machinery ends cleanly in the relationship). The catalogue's closing note: every case above is rare at eleven people and certain across eleven years — and the firm that installed the conventions in peacetime meets each one as a form to fill, while the firm that didn't meets each one as a crisis to improvise, in front of exactly the person least able to extend patience: the catalogue is this module's quiet insurance, and it costs a policy page.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's exits year", "16", "<p>The module's machinery, tested by the year that eventually comes. Every chapter clocks in.</p><p><b>The accountant's settlement (Ch. 2-4).</b> HR 8's calm exit reaches this desk: the separation package lands complete (last day, +6 leave days, no notice issues, no open instruments), the templated statement computes in an hour (the proration, the leave valuation at the stated basis, the final statutory layer — the register closing clean), the walk happens at the exit interview's tail (twenty minutes, two questions, both answered by pointing at lines), the discharge signs, and the settlement pays on day 6 of a published 14-day policy — the leaver's line from HR 8 (<i>smoothest exit I've had anywhere</i>) now has its second half: the settlement statement she showed her new employer's HR <i>as an example of how it should look</i> — the reference commons fed from the settlement desk, exactly as Chapter 1 promised.</p><p><b>The retirement (Ch. 5).</b> The senior Ibadan storekeeper reaches the terms' retirement age: the final contributions verified remitted inside the seven days (the fiduciary stream's last delivery, checked by name), the RSA handover oriented (the PFA meeting arranged, the contribution history printout in hand — his register, complete, every month of the structured era matched), the group life confirmed in force through the last day, the settlement's ordinary components running the template — and the ceremony carrying the day: the long-service recognition (the policy adopted THIS year, applied evenly from its terms), the send-off at the branch, the years named. His comment enters the file: <i>I watched three men retire from my old employer with nothing but arguments about their money. I retired with a statement and a party.</i></p><p><b>The hardest case (Ch. 6).</b> The year brings what years eventually bring: a delivery driver dies in a road accident, off duty, mid-year. The procedure — written eight months earlier in peacetime — carries the firm: the run's validation holds him from the payment file the same week (the estate channel engaged instead), the group life claim initiated in FOUR days (the record layer producing every document the insurer asks for in one afternoon — the nomination current from the annual verification, which spares the family a dispute nobody needed), the PFA death-benefit process supported (the contribution register complete through the final month — the family's starting point clean), the estate settlement computed by the template and HELD for the legal channel (the adviser engaged day one; the family told the process and the honest timeline in the first week — the named senior contact keeping one voice throughout), the funeral attended, the colleagues supported through the HR track's channels. The claim pays; the estate settles through the letters of administration; and the widow's letter to the MD enters the file in full. Its second sentence is the chapter's thesis, written by its beneficiary: <i>you were the only part of this that worked the way people promise things will work.</i></p><p><b>The maternity case (Ch. 7).</b> HR 4's months-ahead machinery meets this module's pricing: the till supervisor's maternity leave priced in writing before it begins (each month's pay stated — the statutory floor confirmed with the adviser, the firm's policy meeting it), the affected slips running per the treatment without a single query (the announcement having pre-answered all of them), the loan schedule paused per its instrument for the reduced months and resumption dated, the return month ordinary. The year's file note from the MD closes the case study: <i>exits used to be where our systems ended and our arguments began; this year they were where the systems mattered most — and the arguments never came.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the seventh law", "13", "<p>The exit layer's scar tissue. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the settlement negotiated at the door.</b> Symptom: the F&amp;F haggled in the exit week; both sides anchoring from grievance; the amount settling by exhaustion. Disease: no ledgers, no conventions, no template — the inputs arriving as recollections (Ch. 1-3). Fix: the machinery's settlement — HR 7's package consumed, the templated statement, the walk, the discharge: the negotiation dissolved by arithmetic.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the leave balance from memory.</b> Symptom: the exit's biggest argument is days; the leaver's count and the firm's count strangers; the difference split in resentment. Disease: HR 4's ledger absent (Ch. 2). Fix: the ledger's figure final and the valuation basis stated — a number read, not negotiated.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the loan surprise.</b> Symptom: the balance deducted without the clause; the leaver blindsided; or the balance forgotten and the firm's money walking out. Disease: module 4's separation clause missing or the three-way reconciliation never run (Ch. 2-3). Fix: the clause in every instrument at lending, the balance confirmed against slip-register-ledger before the settlement consumes it.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the estate case fumbled.</b> Symptom: the deceased in a payment file; the claim initiated late or never; the entitlements paid to the loudest relative; the family's grief compounded by the firm's improvisation. Disease: no procedure, written in peacetime (Ch. 6). Fix: the deceased-employee procedure on the shelf — statuses, claims, channels, one named voice, the adviser day one — rigour and humanity, simultaneously.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the special case improvised.</b> Symptom: maternity pay decided per case; the unpaid month hand-hacked; the compound month botched; each edge a fresh argument with a vulnerable person. Disease: conventions never stated (Ch. 7). Fix: the catalogue's policy page — the treatments configured, the transitions announced, every edge a form to fill.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the settlement that never comes.</b> Symptom: the F&amp;F promised and deferred; the leaver calling monthly; the firm's name spending in the trade with every call. Disease: no deadline, or one set dishonestly (Ch. 4). Fix: the published timeline the firm can meet, met every time — the undisputed bulk paid on schedule even when a line argues, and the exit file closing the account the way it opened: on a date, in writing.</p><p><b>The seventh law.</b> The structure computes, the state rides, the run seals, the account renders, the one-offs join, the changes govern — and now the account closes: <b>every exit computes once — from documents, on a published deadline, through separated hands, with a statement walked, a discharge signed, a file complete, and dignity throughout; the terminal natures per their confirmed law, the hardest case per the procedure written in peacetime, and every edge per a convention stated before it was needed.</b> The exit is where the whole track testifies at once — and the firm whose exits are boring has passed the trilogy's final exam in public, every time someone leaves. One module remains: the structural layer — settings, controls, the payroll calendar, and the function that survives its people — closing the track and the six-certification academy.</p>"),
]

